  'scripted.exit_104': 'Invalid k6 configuration',
  'scripted.exit_105': 'Aborted by an external signal',
  'scripted.exit_106': 'k6 REST API port could not be opened',
  'scripted.exit_107': 'Script runtime error',
  'scripted.exit_108': 'Script stopped via test.abort()',
  'scripted.exit_109': 'k6 internal error (panic)',
  'scripted.exit_neg1': 'Process could not start, or was terminated',
  'scripted.exit_unknown': 'Unknown exit code ({0})',
  'scripted.exitHelp_104': 'Fix the options/CLI configuration — the target may well be healthy.',
  'scripted.exitHelp_105': 'k6 was not terminated by us: this may be a pod restart, a memory (OOM) limit or a node eviction.',
  'scripted.exitHelp_106': 'Concurrent k6 processes may be clashing over the port; lower the pool size.',
  'scripted.exitHelp_109': 'k6 crashed internally — check its release notes.',
  'scripted.hintOldEngine': 'This may not be a typo: the k6 {0} in this environment cannot parse modern JavaScript syntax — `?.`, `??` and `{...object}` are unsupported. Write them with classic `&&` / `||` and `Object.assign`. If the script runs on another machine, this is most likely why.',
  'scripted.hintTimeoutNoDetail': 'The process was killed on timeout before k6 could report a reason: k6\'s own default request timeout is also 60s, so the request never fails on its own. Give the request an EXPLICIT, shorter timeout in the script — e.g. `http.post(url, body, { headers: headers, timeout: \'20s\' })`. k6 then finishes cleanly and reports the real cause (connection refused, DNS, TLS, request timeout).',
  'scripted.hintTimeoutRunDiagnostics': 'The script sets an explicit request timeout and it is shorter than the process budget — so k6 had time to report a reason but did not. At this point the problem is not in the script but in EGRESS: run the probe for this monitor\'s URL from the "Connection Diagnostics" tab. It tries the same target with/without the corporate proxy and with/without the corporate CA bundle; whichever leg succeeds tells you where the block is.',
  'scripted.hintRequestTimeout': 'The request hit k6\'s own timeout (60s unless the script sets one; INDEPENDENT of the monitor process timeout). Two possibilities: (1) the target really is slow — raise the script `timeout` and the monitor timeout (max 180s); (2) egress goes through a proxy/firewall — TCP connects but no response ever arrives. For the latter, check the run\'s "Corporate proxy" setting: the same pod may be doing certificate/RDAP checks through a proxy.',
