  // ── Page Speed ──
  'pspd.iv5m': '5 min', 'pspd.iv15m': '15 min', 'pspd.iv30m': '30 min',
  'pspd.iv1h': '1 hr', 'pspd.iv6h': '6 hr', 'pspd.iv24h': '24 hr',
  'pspd.title':    'Page Speed Monitoring',
  'pspd.subtitle': 'Measures how long a page takes to load and how heavy it has become: time to first byte, total load time, transfer size and request count.',
  'pspd.typeInfo': 'Page Speed measures the WEIGHT and TIMING of a page. Whether the page is up at all is what HTTP monitoring answers; whether its resources are broken is what Page Integrity answers.',
  'pspd.howTitle': 'How and where are these checks done?',
  'pspd.how1': 'Each measurement starts by fetching the page HTML: the time from sending the request to the first byte arriving is recorded as TTFB (how long the server took to think), and the time until the body finishes as the HTML time.',
  'pspd.how2': 'The sub-resources a browser would actually load (img/srcset, CSS, JS, iframes, fonts, favicon) are then extracted from the HTML and fetched one by one with bounded concurrency, each timed and weighed. Total load time is the wall clock for all of that.',
  'pspd.how3': 'a[href] links are NOT measured: a browser does not download them, they are navigation targets. Counting them would make the page look heavier than it is.',
  'pspd.how4': 'IMPORTANT LIMIT: no real browser is involved, so JavaScript does NOT run. Core Web Vitals such as LCP and CLS are therefore not claimed, and resources injected later by scripts are not counted. What is measured is how fast the server responds and how heavy the page is.',
  'pspd.how5': 'Requests go DIRECTLY from the pod by default. That is a matter of accuracy rather than consistency: a measurement taken through a proxy charges the proxy\'s own latency to the page. The form\'s “Corporate proxy” setting (Same as inventory / Always via proxy) can turn the proxy on for pages that are unreachable without it; the phase breakdown (DNS/TCP/TLS) is then not measured and shows —.',
  'pspd.how6': 'Breaching any of the four thresholds (load time, TTFB, size, request count) raises a PERFORMANCE alert. That is NOT an outage and does not affect the uptime figure. If the page cannot be fetched at all, a separate outage alert is raised.',
  'pspd.how7': 'The resource breakdown is always kept for the latest measurement, and kept permanently for moments when a threshold was breached — so "why was it slow last Tuesday?" can still be answered afterwards.',
  'pspd.autoRefresh': 'Auto refresh: {0}s',
